{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792310672,"nonce":"cbPgRPN8zambHMhrWIqEdDZ3nVE=","title":"Loading a native-lib auto-defines a flag per plugin, which changes cache_discriminator, so the carried stdlib snapshot never matches for ...","message":"**MEASURED 2026-08-17 at `64993625`.** Trivial `Sys.println` program, same\nproject, only difference is `[build] native-libs`:\n\n| config | cold wall | cache |\n|---|---|---|\n| no native-libs | **0.08s** | `cache_hit=42 cache_miss=0` |\n| 2 native-libs | **0.32s** | `cache_hit=0 cache_miss=51` |\n\n**IT IS NOT dlopen.** Both dylibs are \u003c1MB, and the profile shows the time in\n`tc.imports 275ms` — the library being lowered from source again, not in\nlibrary loading. The cause:\n\n`src/main.rs` ~1708 auto-defines a flag per LOADED plugin (\"so library code can\nguard an optional backend with `#if gpu` and the define can never disagree with\nreality\"). Those defines feed `CompilationConfig::cache_discriminator()`, so:\n\n    no plugins   -\u003e native-b005eca1   (in the snapshot)\n    2 plugins    -\u003e native-df49b359   (NOT in the snapshot)\n\nThe snapshot carries only the three plain tier discriminators\n(jit/llvm/interp). So **every project with native-libs misses the snapshot\nentirely** — which means nue, the main consumer, gets none of the cold-start\nwork. The lookup misses on the KEY, before any hash check: `RAYZOR_DEBUG_BLADE`\nprints nothing at all, which is the signature of a key miss rather than a\nstale entry.\n\n**PARTIAL EVIDENCE FOR THE FIX, NOT YET CONCLUSIVE.** **0** haxe-std files\nbranch on any plugin-derived define (`tensors`, `nue`, `gpu`) — checked with a\ngrep over every `#if/#elseif` condition — so preprocessing cannot differ. BUT\nbyte-comparing the stdlib artifacts lowered with vs without plugin defines:\n**16 of 42 identical, 26 differing** after normalising the header fields. The\n26 may be id renumbering from the extra plugin registrations rather than\nsemantic difference — NOT DETERMINED. Settle that before keying anything on it.\n\n**NORMALISE BEFORE COMPARING `.blade` FILES — three fields encode the key and\nthe clock, not content.** After the `source_path` string come three LEB128\nvarints: `source_hash`, `source_timestamp`, `compile_timestamp`. `source_hash`\nfolds the defines, so it differs by construction whenever the discriminator\ndoes; the timestamps are `now` and differ between sequential runs. Zero all\nthree, then compare. (This confound produced two wrong conclusions in one\nsession — first \"12 modules differ\" which was clock skew, then \"all 42 differ\"\nwhich was the key.)\n\n**RESOLVED 2026-08-17 — the exclusion is UNSOUND. Do not ship it.**\n\nChain of evidence:\n1. Lowering is DETERMINISTIC: two runs of the same config give byte-identical\n   artifacts (42/42). So differences across configs are real, not noise.\n2. The differing modules carry the SAME SYMBOL STRINGS — extracting all\n   printable runs from `Math`/`String`/`Sys` gives identical sets either way.\n   Nothing binds to a plugin symbol.\n3. But sizes differ by exactly **1 byte** with ~90% of bytes differing: the\n   signature of ONE varint changing length and displacing everything after it.\n   An id base is one larger when plugins are registered.\n\nSo plugin registration shifts the artifact's embedded IDs. Those ids are\ncontext-relative, and a restored module whose ids were assigned under a\ndifferent plugin set is precisely the id-space hazard this codebase keeps\nhitting ([[bugs-stdtypes-type-addition-breaks-nue]],\n[[bugs-interface-dispatch-symbolid-drift-fix]]). Memory already warned that\n\"only N bytes differ after the metadata varint cascade\" reasoning was WRONG\nonce — same trap, do not fall for it again.\n\n**dlopen IS NOT A COST — measured.** Warm, everything cached: 0.05s with two\nnative-libs, 0.05s without. Identical. Lazy-loading plugins would not buy\ncold-start time directly; it would only help by removing the auto-define, and\nit would ALSO change stdlib lowering (the ids shift for the opposite reason),\nso it is not a free win either.\n\n**WHAT PLUGIN PROJECTS ALREADY GET** from `64993625`: the FIRST program in a\nfresh project lowers the library once (0.32s); every program after it in that\nproject gets `cache_hit=42` and 0.06s. Only the first compile is uncovered.\n\n**THE ONLY SOUND ROUTE** is the documented structural one: stop the lowered\npayload carrying context-relative ids\n([[perf-bsym-manifest-is-net-negative-on-cold-start]] enumerates the leaks).\nUntil then the snapshot cannot serve a project whose plugin set differs from\nthe generator's, and that is correct behaviour rather than a bug to route\naround.\n\n---\nArchived from Claude memory `bugs_plugin_defines_defeat_the_stdlib_snapshot` on 2026-10-18. A record of past work; open items in it may have their own issues.","files":null}]}